<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Sogn</h1><p class="st-meta">750 CE – 880 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Petty kingdom around the Sognefjord, Norway's longest fjord, absorbed into Harald Fairhair's realm in the late 9th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Sogn, the land around the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sognefjord">Sognefjord</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, appears in the sagas as one of the small kingdoms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>. Rich graves along the fjord show a prosperous elite in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, but its rulers are known only from late Icelandic tradition.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its people farmed the fjord slopes, raised livestock in mountain pastures and traded along the coast and over the mountains to eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. The site of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulating">Gulating</a>, the great law assembly of western Norway, lay at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulen">Gulen</a> near the mouth of the fjord, and the district later belonged to its law province.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The sagas say <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a> took Sogn during his conquest of the west coast and placed it under one of his earls. It became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway</a>; in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> Sogn was known for its stave churches, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urnes-stave-church">Urnes</a>, now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sogn.jpg" alt="Sogn" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Pjacklam · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hafslovatnet_Norway.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>750 CE – 880 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Norse paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Norse</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Sogn, Sygnafylke</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=815&amp;focus=sogn" aria-label="Sogn on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4821 138 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sogn lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5141 394L5225 425"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5225 425h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5141 394h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5141 394h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=815&amp;focus=sogn">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
